WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:08.720
LangGraph Deep Dive is a workbench
of twenty-three LangGraph examples.

2
00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.160
Each one solves a concrete problem,

3
00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.270
from routing support
tickets to approving loans,

4
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.110
and all of them load
into one local server.

5
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.100
This video shows how they actually run.

6
00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.340
Every example is built
from the same four ideas.

7
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.130
State is a set of typed fields
that every step can read.

8
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.020
A node is a plain function that reads
that state and returns an update.

9
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.360
An edge says what runs next,

10
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.150
and a conditional edge lets the
program decide at run time.

11
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.280
Open any example in Studio,
press Submit on a blank form,

12
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.458
and a ready-made case runs, so you
never need to know the data first.

13
00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:51.167
Here is a real run, calling a real model.

14
00:00:51.647 --> 00:00:54.487
A support ticket says the
plug smells of burning.

15
00:00:55.027 --> 00:00:59.417
The load ticket node reads it, and
triage asks the model to classify it.

16
00:01:00.077 --> 00:01:03.607
Then a conditional edge picks
exactly one of six next steps,

17
00:01:03.827 --> 00:01:06.757
and this one is escalate,
not a specialist.

18
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:08.957
Every step wrote a checkpoint,

19
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:12.667
so the whole run can be
inspected, or replayed, later.

20
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:15.577
Some work should run in parallel,

21
00:01:15.987 --> 00:01:18.517
but you only know how much
once you have planned it.

22
00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:24.107
In the report orchestrator, one node
plans the sections, and the Send API

23
00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:28.067
launches a separate write section task
for each of them at the same time.

24
00:01:28.627 --> 00:01:31.847
A reducer then merges the
drafts into one list,

25
00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:34.467
and the graph carries on
to write the summary,

26
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:36.992
and check every number against the data.

27
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.560
Loops are simply edges
that point backwards.

28
00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.980
Here a writer drafts a
product announcement.

29
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.650
A rule checker written in code, and a
critic model, review it side by side,

30
00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.660
and a judge decides whether
to pass, or go round again.

31
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.370
This brief is impossible on purpose:

32
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.920
sixteen words of required
facts, in a ten word limit.

33
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.760
So the loop runs to its
cap of three drafts,

34
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.900
and stops, honestly labelled best effort.

35
00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.700
Some decisions need a person.

36
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.620
This loan screener asks
the OpenAI Decisions API

37
00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.560
for calibrated probabilities, then
applies policy in plain code.

38
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.290
For a borderline file, the
underwriter node calls interrupt.

39
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.720
The whole state is saved in a
checkpoint under the thread I D,

40
00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.080
and nothing runs until someone answers.

41
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.900
A Command carrying a resume value
wakes the same thread exactly

42
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.800
where it stopped, and the
decision flows on to the letter.

43
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.380
Streaming lets you watch, instead of wait.

44
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.760
The review digest loads the reviews,

45
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:41.470
runs three analysis stages inside a
subgraph, and then writes the summary.

46
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.270
While it works, the server streams every
event as it happens: tasks starting,

47
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.130
checkpoints saved, custom progress
messages from inside the nodes,

48
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.190
and the digest itself, token by token.

49
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.344
Everything on this screen is
drawn from that one stream.

50
00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.333
Memory comes in two kinds.

51
00:02:59.573 --> 00:03:03.483
Short term memory is the thread: the
checkpointer keeps the conversation,

52
00:03:03.793 --> 00:03:08.213
and when it grows too long, the graph
folds the old messages into a summary,

53
00:03:08.513 --> 00:03:10.463
while pinning facts like an order number.

54
00:03:11.143 --> 00:03:12.963
Long term memory is the Store.

55
00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:17.743
The memory concierge saves notes about
a customer, and a later conversation,

56
00:03:17.903 --> 00:03:20.883
on a different thread, recalls
them with semantic search.

57
00:03:21.877 --> 00:03:24.157
LangGraph Studio shows all of this.

58
00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:28.417
Point it at the local server, and
every example appears as a graph.

59
00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:31.087
Choose a ready-made
case from the dropdown,

60
00:03:31.247 --> 00:03:35.117
press Submit, and the graph
follows each step as it runs.

61
00:03:35.977 --> 00:03:38.837
Then click any step to
inspect exactly what it read

62
00:03:38.877 --> 00:03:41.217
and wrote, including
what the model returned.

63
00:03:41.777 --> 00:03:44.547
Or go back to any checkpoint,
re-run from there,

64
00:03:44.797 --> 00:03:47.067
and fork the thread to
take a different turn.

65
00:03:48.137 --> 00:03:52.277
Twenty-three graphs, ready-made cases
for everything that needs data,

66
00:03:52.587 --> 00:03:56.327
and an offline mode, so the whole
test suite runs without a key.

67
00:03:56.927 --> 00:04:02.507
Start the server with U V run langgraph
dev, pick a case, and watch it run.

